        <h2>Backlog Recovery Series</h2>

        <p class="feature-week-byline">
          How to Recover from a Proposal Losing Streak Without Panicking
        </p>

        <div class="feature-week-author">
          <img
            src="/images/michele2.png"
            alt="Michele Rochon-Wood"
            class="feature-week-author-image"
          >

          <div class="feature-week-author-details">
            <div class="feature-week-author-label">
              AUTHOR
            </div>

            <div class="feature-week-author-name">
              Michele Rochon-Wood
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          Every consulting team loses proposals. Sometimes those losses arrive one at a time, quietly and predictably. Other times, they arrive in clusters. A team works hard, prepares carefully, talks to the client, submits strong work, and still watches several opportunities disappear in succession.
        </p>

        <p>
          That kind of losing streak can affect more than backlog. It can drain confidence, create anxiety, and tempt people to respond in ways that make the problem worse. Some teams start chasing every RFP that appears. Others assume every loss came down to price. Some retreat from business development altogether because the disappointment feels too fresh.
        </p>

        <div class="feature-week-pullquote feature-week-animate">
          <p>A proposal losing streak is not a verdict. It is a signal.</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Recover the Right Way</h3>

        <p>
          The Backlog Recovery Series was created for exactly this moment. It gives technical consulting teams a structured way to recover after several proposal losses, learn from what happened, reconnect with clients, and rebuild momentum without wasting more time on weak pursuits.
        </p>

        <p>
          The series begins with a video called <strong>Proposal Losing Streak? Make it a Short One</strong>. The video acknowledges the emotional reality of losing work, but it quickly moves into practical strategy. The objective is not to pretend losses do not matter. The objective is to feel the disappointment, collect the intelligence, and get moving again.
        </p>

        <h3>Start with Debriefs</h3>

        <p>
          A proposal debrief is one of the most valuable business development activities a consulting team can perform. It helps you understand how your submission was received, how your team compared, what the winning firm did well, and what the client may need next.
        </p>

        <p>
          The most important rule is to avoid defensiveness. A debrief is not a chance to argue with the client or convince them they made the wrong decision. It is a chance to listen carefully, ask better questions, and leave with information your team can use.
        </p>

        <p>
          Ask about your project understanding. Ask whether your work plan felt practical. Ask how your team compared. Ask whether price influenced the decision, but do not let price dominate the conversation. Most importantly, ask about future work. A lost proposal may still lead to valuable intelligence about upcoming opportunities.
        </p>

        <div class="feature-week-pullquote feature-week-animate">
          <p>The loss is behind you. The intelligence is still useful.</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Hold a Backlog Recovery Workshop</h3>

        <p>
          After the debriefs are complete, the next step is to bring the team together for a Backlog Recovery Workshop. This is not a blame session. It is a collaborative review of recent proposal losses designed to identify patterns that would be hard to see when each pursuit is reviewed separately.
        </p>

        <p>
          Put the RFPs, submissions, debrief notes, and pursuit planning information side by side. Look for recurring weaknesses. Did the project understanding sections miss important details? Were the work plans too generic? Were the same competitors showing up repeatedly? Did your team pursue opportunities where the client relationship was weak from the beginning?
        </p>

        <p>
          The workshop also includes a little theatrical relief: a proposal funeral. It may sound silly, but it serves a purpose. Teams need a way to acknowledge the disappointment before they can analyze it honestly. A little humour lowers defensiveness and makes the learning more memorable.
        </p>

        <h3>Get Back into Business Development Mode</h3>

        <p>
          Once the team has learned from the losses, recovery becomes active. This is where the accompanying prompt set comes in. It guides participants through twenty practical activities that rebuild business development momentum over the following month.
        </p>

        <p>
          The first set of prompts helps technical consultants get their heads back into business development mode. This includes reviewing contacts, watching client organizations, setting weekly goals, and identifying opportunities that may have been overlooked while the team was focused on proposal production.
        </p>

        <h3>Reconnect with Clients</h3>

        <p>
          The second set of prompts focuses on calling clients and refreshing relationships. Sometimes a small project can be triggered by a simple call. Sometimes the value is market intelligence. Sometimes the conversation reopens a relationship that has been quiet for too long.
        </p>

        <p>
          These calls should not feel desperate. They should feel curious, professional, and useful. The purpose is to understand what clients are dealing with, what work may be coming, and where your team might be genuinely helpful.
        </p>

        <h3>Show Up in the Market</h3>

        <p>
          The third set of prompts focuses on industry events. Local events, association meetings, technical sessions, charity events, golf tournaments, and even informal gatherings can help teams reconnect with clients and partners.
        </p>

        <p>
          The key is to attend with intention. Research the event. Identify who may be there. Prepare conversation starters. Record what you learn afterward. Complete follow-up within a few days. Events only become business development assets when the information and relationships are captured.
        </p>

        <h3>Choose Pursuits More Carefully</h3>

        <p>
          The final set of prompts helps teams qualify pursuits more carefully. After a losing streak, proposal resources are precious. Teams cannot afford to chase every opportunity just because backlog is thin.
        </p>

        <p>
          Stronger pursuit decisions require better questions. Do we know the client? Do we understand the project? Can we differentiate ourselves? Is this a price-driven competition? Are we in the centre of our wheelhouse? If the answer is no, the best recovery decision may be to decline and focus elsewhere.
        </p>

        <div class="feature-week-pullquote feature-week-animate">
          <p>Backlog recovery is built one better decision at a time.</p>
        </div>

        <h3>A Complete Recovery System</h3>

        <p>
          The Backlog Recovery Series includes a video, prompt set, workbook, template, and facilitated workshop exercise. Together, these resources help consulting teams move from disappointment to analysis, from analysis to action, and from action to stronger future pursuits.
        </p>

        <p>
          The goal is not simply to win the next proposal. The goal is to build better habits: better debriefs, better client conversations, better event follow-up, better pursuit decisions, and better records of what the team learns.
        </p>

        <p>
          Every proposal leaves something behind. Winning gives you backlog. Losing gives you intelligence. Firms that collect both become stronger over time.
        </p>

